60,3,1,talk,Asuka,,"Option A is the most logical. It addresses the financial deficit @Ryuji mentioned, satisfies the daily riders' willingness to pay, and creates a clear ownership that reduces the shoving matches and congestion @George and I observed.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Ryuji"",""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Ryuji,,"Option A provides a revenue stream, but @Mio's findings about the treasurer's workload are concerning. Adding monthly billing for individual spaces could be an administrative nightmare. I am leaning toward a simpler approach.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,George,,"Option B seems the most practical. If we remove fixed assignments, we eliminate the resentment over who gets which spot and solve the congestion I saw, as people can park where there is actually space.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Shunichi,,"Option B is a recipe for disaster. You're replacing ""quiet resentment"" with ""daily combat"" over the best spots. If we want to avoid a building full of people avoiding each other, we need a clear, fixed rule.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Mio,,"@Asuka, I really can't do Option A! The other block's secretary said fees made people stop coming to meetings, and the treasurer is already working so hard. Let's not make it a nightmare!","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Mio, the current ""free"" system is already a nightmare of congestion and rain-soaked bikes. Option B only worsens this by making every single morning a competition. A paid system provides order and funds for the building.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Asuka, while I see the logic, we cannot ignore the risk of administrative burnout. Option C is the most stable; it requires minimal effort from the treasurer and provides a clear, impartial rule that prevents the ""daily combat"" @Shunichi fears.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Asuka"",""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,George,,"@Ryuji, a lottery is impartial, but it doesn't solve the physical congestion I witnessed. If we simply shuffle the same inefficient spots every year, we are just rotating the frustration without fixing the actual problem of bikes blocking each other.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Shunichi,,"@George, you're still obsessed with the geometry of the bikes. The real problem is the social climate. Option C is boring, but it's a clean break. It's a fair, fixed rule that doesn't turn the lobby into a war zone.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Mio,,"@Ryuji, I totally agree! Option C sounds way easier. No monthly bills to track, no fighting over spots every morning, and no one has to do a mountain of paperwork. Let's just keep it simple and happy!","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Ryuji and @Mio, you are prioritizing administrative ease over actual functionality. Option C ignores the rain and the congestion. If we just rotate the misery, we haven't solved anything. Option A is the only one that provides a sustainable solution.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Ryuji"",""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Ryuji,,"@Asuka, functionality is useless if the system collapses due to administrative failure. Option A risks the treasurer's burnout and resident hostility. Option C is the most reasonable compromise between fairness and sustainability.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,George,,"@Ryuji, I understand the need for sustainability, but Option C is merely a gamble. If we don't address the physical layout, we are just assigning people to a broken system. Option B at least encourages flexibility.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Shunichi,,"@George, flexibility is just a polite word for chaos. Option B is a free-for-all that will turn every morning into a battle of nerves. I'll take a boring lottery over a daily skirmish any day.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Mio,,"@George, but chaos is way more stressful than a lottery! @Asuka, I know you want it perfect, but a ""perfect"" system that makes everyone grumpy or exhausted isn't actually perfect. I'm voting for Option C!","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""George"",""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Asuka,,"@Mio, @Ryuji, you are choosing convenience over a real solution. Option C is a gamble that ignores the physical reality of the rain and congestion. I will vote for Option A to ensure order and funding.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Mio"",""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Ryuji,,"The physical issues are real, but they cannot be solved by a system that is administratively unsustainable. Option C is the only choice that ensures fairness without risking financial conflict or burnout. I vote for Option C.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,George,,"I cannot support a lottery that ignores the physical congestion I witnessed. However, Option B's chaos is also concerning. I will vote for Option B to prioritize flexibility and actual usage over a broken, fixed system.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Shunichi,,"@George, your ""flexibility"" is just a fancy word for a daily brawl. I've seen how this building handles conflict—by ignoring it until it rots. Option C is the only way to keep the peace. I vote for Option C.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Mio,,I'm with @Ryuji and @Shunichi! Option C is the only one that doesn't sound like a total headache for the person in charge or a way to make neighbors fight. I vote for Option C!,"{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Ryuji"",""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
